A||||13||Homicidal||Switching the sub to the front compartment??||||5||
Z||000000||Homicidal||07-29-2000||01:43 PM||||I currently have it in a tight enclosed box behind my seat.  Sounds awesome, really shakes things up.  But would it sound better overall inside the front trunk compartment, with a box similiar to Formula's or another custom style?||||reg||5
Z||000001||86SE||07-29-2000||08:58 PM||||If your Fiero is like mine, you won't hear anything on the inside if your sub is outside.  With my doors, windows, and sunroof shut, you can't hear anything but the low low frequency outside.<P>You should put the sub in the passenger footwell, face it so its fireing INTO the footwell and it will shake the ENTIRE interior of the car!  With equal bass throught the car, so not just the passenger or just the driver 'get' the bass.<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero||||reg||11
Z||000002||Homicidal||07-29-2000||09:48 PM||||Ah, on the contrary.  When I first put the sub in, I had it face towards the seat, so when a low frequency hit, the seat would soak up the entire bass.  While it gave a great back message to me, it gave nothing to the passenger and got annoying.  Not to mention the bass sounded muffled to everyone else and myself.  <P>So I got the "bright" idea to turn it AROUND and face the firewall!  MUCH bigger improvement in overall sound quality.  But it reduced the bass I and the passenger felt to nearly non-existant.  So, remember back to my physics class, I remembered waves need a medium similiar to AIR to travel through to sound the best.  Especially sound waves!  So I put a 1" spacer in between the box and the seat.  MY GOD what 1" can do for sound!  This box is INSIDE the car, and with all the windows up and doors shut, I can STILL feel it OUTSIDE the car!<P>I thought it might be better in the front compartment since it would relatively work like a hatchback if you built the box right, where it would reflect right off the hood when closed.<P>I guess if I grow a few feet I'll HAVE to move it, but for now, I guess it stays!<P>I don't want to put it into the passenger foot well, I already got a 12 cd changer behind the passenger seat, I don't need to restrict the leg room even MORE!  Besides, it would tickle my girlfriend's feet!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula||07-29-2000||11:10 PM||||I can have the windows up and it sounds the same, I do have a hatch style box but, its facing in toward the cab a little, the bass is extremly crisp and clear in the car compared to when I sat it inside, it actually kind of fileters out the noise you dont want to hear, and it DOES shake the car like nobody's business, I need to tighten some interior parts because they are coming lose.  I personally would do this set up again just because you dont lose leg space, but I also like the sub that someone cut a bass tube in half and put it under the dash, its really up to what you want.... if the bass comes from the outside and goes in then you can draw crowds, but if its inside going out then you wont...  btw, it would rip up the drive by SPL class in car audio, it would be in like the 0-100 class with 1 10" and 300watt amp but have outreagous external spl!!!  if you want to be heard than I will tell you now that the 10 outside is a better choice, plus its sound just about right inside.... but if you want thundering bass inside than just put it under the dash||||reg||1
Z||000004||Homicidal||07-31-2000||02:43 PM||||I wish I had a spare box to put my sub in and test it.  I would love to hear what it sounds like.  But if I built a box for the front and I hated it, it's a waste of money and time.||||reg||1
Z||000005||BellaFiero||07-31-2000||03:14 PM||||Homicidal! You wouldn't be doing it for nothing! I'd buy that box off you so quickly… in fact, if anyone can design a box for the front compartment, I'd really like to know how much or how to do it myself. I've never built a box for a sub before, and I have a general idea but no experience on how to go about it. I've got a 10" in a rather large box sitting under the dash of my Bella. However, it's poking out from under the dash a good four inches, and it scrapes the bottom of the dash pocket, that's how high it is. Most of it is hidden, but it still KILLS legroom for the passenger. My fiance says she doesn't mind, but well... I'd like her to be able to sit in the seat without both legs pressed between the box and the door, you know? Any thoughts? It's a huge box, like I said... how could I get it up front? And couldn't you port it somehow... some kind of PVC piping to lead sound into the cabin?||||reg||10
Z||000006||Alex4mula||07-31-2000||03:23 PM||||I have a 1cf box on the passenger footwell with a 8" Rockford sub driven by a 150W amp. Firing towards the front of the car it sounds very good. Firing towards the seat it sounds with very weak with little bass. I have two theories. One is that the sub is not getting enough power for its efficiency (87db). The other is that firing towards the front it adds to the waves of the 4x10 helping each other like in some dual subs setups. Any ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Homicidal||07-31-2000||03:23 PM||||See if this site helps, I can also email you pics of Formula's box and another's custom box.  <P>Basically, you need some wood cutting skills, hopefully you have a friend who does, like I do!<P> <A HREF="http://www.subbox.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.subbox.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||stimpy||07-31-2000||08:15 PM||||Hopefully, by tomorrow I should have pcs of the enclosure I made for my passenger footwell. I think a lot of the problems with fit comes from trying to fit a square peg in a round hole. My enclosure fits the footwell so snugly that no fasteners are eeded to hold it in place, it takes up 99.9% of the space from the side pedal back to the front.  I am using a speaker similar to Homicidals, a Polk dX10 with a 400w amp driving it. It gives very strong and crisp bass with no cutting to the car involved. The legroom loss is negligible unless you have some REALLY long legged folks that you know. At 6'6", I can sit in the passenger seat comfortably.<P>Really, y'all are gonna dig it!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://gifs123.tripod.com/page15/renstimpy2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Formula||07-31-2000||08:23 PM||||hmmmmm.... I'll look into how much it costs to buy the wood and everything and build a box for a ten sometime next week then sell it for the price of the wood and carpet, and the little plastic thing.  I'd say that I spent about $30 on the box for my formula.  Thats everything I think, with 8 gauge wire inside. I also had to buy silicone and a silcone gun and stuff..... I would also check some local pro stereo shops because it is a common size box..... I look into it and give a price and if anyone wants one then I will go buy stuff and build it... it shouldnt be more than $30 with shipping, Im also putting some money in there for labor from a friend.....||||reg||1
Z||000010||86SE||08-01-2000||02:24 AM||||I have my box facing the floor on the passenger side.  With the cone flush to the floor, you get almost no bass and a lot of resistance, but if you raise it 2" (like homicidal) off the floor the bass increases %500.  And thats still with 3 layers of padding its fireing into (floormat, carpet, and insulation.<P>Heres my little theory, I know why its much louder when you turn the sub facing INTO the footwell as opposed to away, much much smaller space, the sound waves have something to bounce off or reflect from producing louder bass as the waves travel through the car.  If you put a metal or plastic plate at the far end of the footwell so the speaker fires directly into it, considerably less waves will be absorbed and more will bounce off the inside of the car producing even lounder bass.<BR>I'll give it a try tomorrow......<P>Second reason why I would not put a sub on the outside, crank it way up......sure it increases your exterior SPL.......decreases your interior SPL.........but most importantly, its advertizing your system.  People will KNOW you got an amp and possibly a good deck inside, they will do what ever it takes to steal it, all of it.  <P>If you put your sub on the outside, turn it WAY down and run some 3 inch tubing to the inside of your car through the air condition system..<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero||||reg||14
Z||000011||BellaFiero||08-01-2000||10:43 AM||||Hey Formula! Does anyone have a pic of your box? And yes, I would be VERY interested in having you make a custom box! That’d be great… let me know!||||reg||10
Z||000012||b lo 0||08-01-2000||10:58 AM||||OK, I'll try and say this without sounding like an a**  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  Tweeters and midranges are very different than subwoofers.  Tweets and mids use waves of air to modulate sound.  Thus, tweeters need no enclosure (neither do some mids)  Subs, however, use changing air pressure.  That's why they need an enclosure, so they can change the pressure of air instead of just moving it around.  If you block off the sub cone, the pressure will not change in the cabin, but just in the enclosure.  Also, as long as you have a properly designed enclosure and amp combo, the direction the cone faces makes no difference (in fact, if you do it right, pointing the cone <I>away</I> from the passengers will sound better, because any distortion you get (waves) will get absorbed by the car and the bass (pressure) will make it around to the passengers.  That's why you can hear bass outside the car but not high range sounds.  Not because it's louder, but because it's a different kind of sound.  My JL 12W6 is mounted behind the passenger seat with 1.5" spacers between the sub and seat.<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT (in progress)  <BR>titled ICE-plates B LO 0 <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.angelfire.com/ga3/audioimagery/ice.gif">   <BR>Go to <A HREF="http://www.audioimagery.i-p.com" TARGET=_blank>www.audioimagery.i-p.com</A>  !!!||||reg||1
